# One arity of `printf`, in its own module.
#
# The header is `<stdio.h>` and the C symbol is `printf` — the same
# symbol `as_text.hero` binds, at a different signature. That is the
# whole point of this fixture: it is legal, it links, and it runs.
#
# `lent`, since panel 171: `printf` reads its format while it runs and
# keeps nothing of it, and without the word a lend at the call is refused,
# because an unmarked C parameter is assumed to keep what it is handed.

extern "stdio.h"
    function printf(format: cstr lent, value: i64) -> i32

# The route out of the module, because an `extern` is never callable
# across a module boundary (acceptance row 1, panel 033 R5). It takes the
# `str` and lends inside itself, which is the shape §4.19 prescribes: a
# wrapper taking `cstr` is a Heroes parameter no mark can reach, and panel
# 171's rule stops a lend there with `lend_needs_a_header`.
function say(format: str, value: i64) -> i32
    return printf(format: format.cstr(), value: value)
